The core whose timer interrupts gives time again only to the threads of its own core,
so a thread of another that waits for its units sits on the spent queue past the count it has time again,
until its own core happens to look; the release of every core lies ahead of the count.

Caught-by: fuzz-smp2
Report: a place waits on another queue than its virtual time says
Witness: fuzz-smp2 cores-release-from-other

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -1007,7 +1007,7 @@ void sched_time(uint64_t now)
     }
     for (uint32_t c = 0; c < CORES; c++) {
         LOOP_BOUND(CORES);
-        if (now >= cores[c].nearest_release) {
+        if (c == core_index() && now >= cores[c].nearest_release) {
             release(c);
         }
     }
